d they made a  series of centrally directed top-down  efforts to promote the development of  the Internet's in China and that  eventually that started out obviously  very heavily reliant on United States  technology and and commercial expertise  but there are a lot of young Chinese  engineers and scientists who had trained  in US and Western universities many done  some kind of apprenticeship in or around  Silicon Valley came back to China imbued  with all sorts of ideas and were very  quickly able to work up a range of  digital services that were uniquely well  suited to the needs of China's emerging  netizen population as it was termed you  had that you you also had extensive  government support for companies like  Huawei and at the same time of course  this became a protected sector that the  you know that foreign businesses could  only get into in a kind of ancillary way  providing technology providing know-how  but not actually doing business as as  such within China and that created a  kind of shield behind which Chinese  companies were able to evolve very  rapidly and is this sort of like a  radical change from the power of  nation-states or just a manifestation or  continuing of other forces well I mean  power is power and you know how it is  defined and how it is exercised changes  over time but if you look at previous  history the way states tended to  exercise power was through the  deliberate use of a calculated use of  military force to achieve their  objectives I think that kind of calculus  of risk if you like has changed since  people have begun to realize just how  devastating industrial and  post-industrial warfare can look like  and what I think this means is that  states are increasingly looking at other  means of exercising power and if you  look at the contest station the contest  that's taking place between the United  States and China at the moment this is a  contest that is first and foremost  playing out in the high technology and  economic arenas and you're in in China's  case they've always had this concept of  comprehensive national power the  necessity of brigade or national  capabilities in pursuit of central  government strategic directives it's  something it's very easy for  authoritarian States to do much less so  for democratic states other than in  extreme situations such as we saw during  World War two so we're seeing China very  deliberately and calculatingly brigade  in all aspects of national power in  pursuit of economic development  political influence and it must be said  military but of course military  capabilities in today's world are  increasingly cyber defendants and cyber  enable the network dependence  and we're seeing the role of what's  rather incorrectly called artificial  intelligence and autonomous systems and  robotics playing a growing role in the  future development of warfare and  China's really pushing its foot to the  floor  to achieve mastery and actually  domination in in these areas because  what's what's going to matter in all  these areas is who gets to decide what  the technical standards are going to be  that everyone else will be required to  rely upon and so it's about you know  creating standards creating control of  networks supply chains or all of these  things that the China is now focused on  and from your own work what are the ways  in which China is like harnessing this  cyber power well what they've done is  pretty much everything they could have  done I think I heard Carrie Gracie on  the BBC of the other day saying they've  paid their they begged borrowed and  stolen their way to the status that they  are now in and that is of course true  they have sought to acquire by various  legitimate means access to most advanced  US and other Western technologies  through purchases through academic  exchanges through sending students to  study these subjects at US universities  etc etc  they've also unquestionably been  involved in very extensive industrial  scale industrial cyber espionage which  is state-directed they're not  necessarily always undertaken by stains  but we're now moving to an interesting  situation where in the past I think  there appeared to be  a conviction in the United States and  elsewhere that China would be able to  copy but not innovate but they've moved  beyond the copy you know the the cut and  paste strategy now and they're showing  that in many areas they they are able to  innovate and I think particularly where  China has shown innovative capability is  maybe not so much in core scientific and  engineering breakthroughs but in the  application of breakthrough technologies  to provide services that offer  efficiency gains that you know  facilitates daily existence and  eradicate a lot of the friction that  exists particularly in Chinese urban  life developing very innovative  approaches to developing the services in  that area I think China has shown a lot  of innovative capability in terms of  artificial intelligence it's so clear  that which is what we really talking  about here is machine learning China is  still some way behind the United States  in pretty much every area but this kind  of machine learning is basically a  function of computing power x data  volume and of course what China is doing  with its pervasive online systems is  constantly generating huge quantities of  data that potentially act as feedstock  for the development of artificial  intelligence programs so the jury's out  on this one but I think a general  expectation is although China lags the  United States at the moment the the the  likelihood is that they will catch up  pretty quickly and we're used to hearing  the internet talked about as a sort of  global good  do you predict that in the future  perhaps in relation to how the Hawaii  case is being dealt with in a moment do  you think we're seeing a move towards a  like nation state controlled internets  of a fragmented world of Internet or a  more global well then I think it's  probably going to be more the former and  already we're seeing signs of that so  China in particular was an early  exponent of internet sovereignty by  which it meant near the ability of the  state to control the content that was  transiting China's physical frontiers  and that was relatively easy to do  because China has for a country its size  extremely few internet international  internet access points very very easy to  get across those and control the flow a  lot of other authoritarian states have  been attracted to this approach  precisely because they see external  ideas as a dangerous challenge to the  regime narrative whatever that is and  that that is a model that is gaining  acceptance in parts of the world we're  seeing now Russia although configured  very differently from China in terms of  internet access points is also trying to  you know bring information in within the  purview of the Russian state if you look  at the Chinese internet it is already a  very distinct ecosystem from the  internet that you and I more habitually  inhabit and utilize you know it really  is it's almost as if you know one has  gone to Mars and found in intelligence  and highly advanced civilization but  doing things rather differently from  from the way that we do and I think that  there is particularly now that there  there is this major standoff between the  United States and China  over the pursuit of technology dominance  in the 21st century I fear that the risk  of a kind of internet bifurcation you  know the evolution into two distinct  systems is a bigger I mean I've been  talking about this for four years as a  possibility but it's looking more like a  possible outcome then I would previously  have thought and what would this look  like would this be result of a cyber war  cyber crisis no not necessarily I think  it would be a consequence of two  different systems going their own way  and in effect you would have a global  internet that was subject to border  controls it's more like that I mean  slightly we talk about cyber conflict  cyber war as if it was something akin to  a kinetic war you know a kind of  exchange of ordnance and armaments in  fact what we've got between these two  competing systems already is a kind of  constant contest station between states  within the cyber domain this is  happening 24/7  everybody's at it the United States has  just come up with this new strategy  called persistent engagement for dealing  with adversaries in the cyber domain  which essentially says you know we're  not going to put up with this anymore  we're going to strike back and whatever  and we're going to do it on on a  continuing you know and incessant basis  which really only  codifies a situation that's been in  existence for 20-plus years already  have you seen we've like we've talked a  lot about war or crisis but have you  seen steps towards something that could  create cyber peace or is there like  structures and which would have to be in  place for this to happen well I mean the  cyber domain is a huge domain and you  have to break it down into many  component parts and the idea that you  can't have one kind of overarching  process that would be able to get its  arms around everything and come to some  sort of globally meaningful conclusion  is I think unrealistic I think what  you've got to do is work in you know the  different sectors and try to aggregate  that up to some broad principles which  can be agreed by all parties a lot of  work is going on doing that I mean one  of the things that I am have been  involved in for the last two years is  called the global Commission for the  stability of cyberspace which has been  funded by a number of governments and  with some private sector involvement  looking at the challenges to the  stability of cyberspace by what  effectively mean we mean the reliable  reliable access to in Internet that we  can trust the you know the the number of  challenges to that situation are high  and rising as we see for example States  increasingly involved in exploits that  involve attacking the domain name system  which is the nearest thing to the the  backbone of the global Internet so  that's just one small example of a lot  of initiatives that are taking place at  different levels at a government level  at an international level through the  United Nations the United Nations  secretary-general Guterres himself with  a background in cybernetic engineering  is very very in  and concerned about this we've had  exercises going on for a number of years  looking at issues of cyber security  under the aegis of the United Nations  first committee which is about peace and  security the so called governmental  group of experts I mean I know there are  lots of governmental groups of experts  under the United Nations but this one is  the only one that deals with  cybersecurity issues we've now got two  processes under the UN first committee  dealing with cybersecurity and they kind  of you know potentially at odds with  each other but plans are afoot to try  and make them work together but unless  and until States can be and it's  particularly true for states can be  persuaded that the costs of carrying on  as we are now will in a long term exceed  the likely benefits I think this is  going to be a very tough ask to deliver  on and that's before we get to issues  like pervasive cybersecurity a criminal  cyber malign activity which is growing  almost exponentially for more debates  talks and interviews subscribe today to  the Institute of Arts and ideas at IAI  TV  you

